{
  "id": "ep-04-03",
  "slug": "difference-of-squares",
  "title": "Difference of Squares",
  "courseId": "exponents-polynomials",
  "chapterId": "ch4-factoring",
  "minutes": 11,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "figure": "foil-box",
      "body": "A **difference of two squares** factors specially: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Recognize two perfect squares with a minus between them — there's no middle term to split, because the outer and inner products cancel."
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Find the second number.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "binomialAreaLab",
        "pX": 1,
        "qX": 1,
        "targetA": 3,
        "targetB": -3,
        "asks": "middle",
        "startA": 0,
        "startB": 0,
        "requiredMoves": 3,
        "prompt": "Build (x + 3)(x − 3) as a rectangle and watch what happens to the middle strips.",
        "successFeedback": "+3 and −3: the strips are +3x and −3x, and they cancel. That cancellation is the whole difference-of-squares pattern — x² − 9 has no middle term because the two partitions are opposites.",
        "productMiddleFeedback": "The corner is right but the strips are not cancelling. For a difference of squares the two partitions must be opposites.",
        "partialFeedback": "Keep dragging toward +3 and −3.",
        "signFeedback": "One partition must be positive and the other negative — that is what makes the middle vanish."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k1",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "A bigger square.",
      "conceptTag": "factor-difference-squares",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "25 = 5², so a = 5: (x + 5)(x − 5).",
        "The square root of 25 is 5, so a = 5."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "Factor (x^2 - 25) as (x + 5)(x - a). Find a.",
        "answer": 5,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 25,
            "feedback": "25 is b², not b. Since 5² = 25, a = 5."
          },
          {
            "value": 10,
            "feedback": "That's 2·5. You need the square root of 25, which is 5."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "25 = 5², so a = 5."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "a1-polynomials",
        "form": "factor-difference-squares__numeric"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Worked: x² − 9. Both x² and 9 are perfect squares (9 = 3²), separated by a minus. So x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3). Multiply back: the middle −3x + 3x cancels, leaving x² − 9.",
      "figure": "ep-diff-squares"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Pick the factors.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "mcq",
        "prompt": "Factor (x^2 - 36).",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "(x + 6)(x - 6)",
            "correct": true,
            "feedback": "Yes — 36 = 6², so x² − 36 = (x + 6)(x − 6)."
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "(x - 6)(x - 6)",
            "feedback": "That's (x − 6)², which gives x² − 12x + 36. For x² − 36, use (x + 6)(x − 6)."
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "(x + 6)(x + 6)",
            "feedback": "That's (x + 6)² = x² + 12x + 36. A difference of squares needs opposite signs."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "A **coefficient** can be a square too: 4x² = (2x)². So 4x² − 9 = (2x)² − 3² = (2x + 3)(2x − 3). Take the square root of each square to get a and b.",
      "figure": "ep-coeff-square"
    },
    {
      "id": "i3",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Square-root the coefficient.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "Factor (4x^2 - 9) as (2x + 3)(2x - a). Find a.",
        "answer": 3,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 9,
            "feedback": "9 is b². Since 3² = 9, a = 3."
          },
          {
            "value": 6,
            "feedback": "That's 2·3. You need the square root of 9, which is 3."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "9 = 3², so a = 3: (2x + 3)(2x − 3)."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k2",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "Now build it yourself, not just pick it.",
      "conceptTag": "factor-difference-squares",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "49 = 7², so x² − 49 = (x + 7)(x − 7).",
        "The a² − b² pattern with a = x, b = 7."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "buildExpression",
        "prompt": "Build the factored form of x^2 - 49.",
        "tokens": [
          {
            "id": "t0",
            "label": "(x + 7)"
          },
          {
            "id": "t1",
            "label": "(x - 7)"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "t0",
          "t1"
        ],
        "acceptAlso": [
          [
            "t1",
            "t0"
          ]
        ],
        "commonBuilds": [
          {
            "sequence": [
              "t1",
              "t1"
            ],
            "feedback": "That's (x − 7)² = x² − 14x + 49. Use opposite signs: (x + 7)(x − 7)."
          },
          {
            "sequence": [
              "t0",
              "t0"
            ],
            "feedback": "That's (x + 7)². A difference of squares needs one plus and one minus."
          }
        ],
        "reusable": true,
        "missFeedback": "A difference of squares splits into a CONJUGATE pair: one +, one −.",
        "successFeedback": "Yes — 49 = 7², so (x + 7)(x − 7)."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "a1-polynomials",
        "form": "factor-difference-squares__buildExpression"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k3",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "Coefficient squared.",
      "conceptTag": "factor-difference-squares",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "4x² = (2x)² and 9 = 3², so (2x + 3)(2x − 3).",
        "The a² − b² pattern with a = 2x, b = 3."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "buildExpression",
        "prompt": "Factor (4x^2 - 9).",
        "tokens": [
          {
            "id": "t0",
            "label": "(2x + 3)"
          },
          {
            "id": "t1",
            "label": "(2x - 3)"
          },
          {
            "id": "t2",
            "label": "(4x + 3)"
          },
          {
            "id": "t3",
            "label": "(x - 3)"
          }
        ],
        "correct": [
          "t0",
          "t1"
        ],
        "acceptAlso": [
          [
            "t1",
            "t0"
          ]
        ],
        "commonBuilds": [
          {
            "sequence": [
              "t1",
              "t1"
            ],
            "feedback": "That's (2x − 3)² = 4x² − 12x + 9. Use opposite signs: (2x + 3)(2x − 3)."
          },
          {
            "sequence": [
              "t2",
              "t3"
            ],
            "feedback": "That doesn't multiply back to 4x² − 9. The square roots are 2x and 3: (2x + 3)(2x − 3)."
          },
          {
            "sequence": [
              "t3",
              "t2"
            ],
            "feedback": "That doesn't multiply back to 4x² − 9. The square roots are 2x and 3: (2x + 3)(2x − 3)."
          }
        ],
        "reusable": true,
        "missFeedback": "Root each square: √(4x²) = 2x and √9 = 3, in a conjugate pair.",
        "successFeedback": "Yes — (2x)² − 3² = 4x² − 9."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "a1-polynomials",
        "form": "factor-difference-squares__buildExpression"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ch1",
      "kind": "challenge",
